course: money
lesson: 41
title: Olympiad: best buys and change at the till ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson mixes the shop lessons of the course - prices, change, offers and unit prices - into puzzles where one careful step after another cracks each one.
explain: The golden rule of a best buy is to compare the same amount: four juice cartons of 250 ml at 40p make a litre for £1.60, while the 1 litre bottle is £1.50 - the bottle wins, and the giant 2 litre bottle at £3.20 does not.
explain: Picture a jacket at £40 with a sign "half price, then a further quarter off": half leaves £20, and a quarter of £20 is £5, so you pay £15, not the £10 that "three quarters off" would give.
explain: Change is easiest by counting up from the price, like the shopkeeper: for £6.35 paid with £10, add 5p to £6.40, 10p to £6.50, 50p to £7 and £3 to £10 - that is £3.65 back.
explain: Watch the talk around the numbers: "the queue was a mile long" is not a number to use, "not ideal" may mean a deal is worse than it looks, and when someone corrects themselves the last number counts.
fact: The unit price is the price for the same amount, such as 100 g, 1 litre or one item; comparing unit prices shows the best buy.
fact: The biggest pack is often cheaper per unit, but not always, so always do the sum.
fact: UK coins are 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1 and £2; notes are £5, £10, £20 and £50; there is no 30p or £3 coin.
fact: To give change in the fewest coins, take the biggest coin that fits again and again: £2.80 is £2, 50p, 20p and 10p, four coins.
fact: Counting up from the price to the money paid gives the change and is a good way to check it.
fact: Two discounts one after the other are not added: half price and then a quarter off the new price takes £20 off a £40 price and then £5 more, leaving £15, which is more than "three quarters off" (£10).
fact: "Buy one, get the second half price" on two £6 books means £6 plus £3, which is £9.
fact: A quarter of a price is the price divided by 4, a fifth is divided by 5, and a tenth is divided by 10.
fact: "£5 off for every £100" on a £300 TV saves 3 lots of £5, which is £15.
fact: A price like £1.99 is 1p less than £2, so three items at £1.99 cost £6 minus 3p, which is £5.97.
fact: If you get too much change, the honest thing is to hand it back; if you get too little, say so politely.
fact: Slang in this lesson: "skint" means having no money, "canny" (North East England) means good or clever, "lush" (Wales) means great, "servo" (Australia) is a petrol station, "bussin'" means really good, "no cap" means honestly, and "sound" (Liverpool, Manchester) means fine or right.
exercise: Pens come in packs of 5 for £2 or packs of 2 for 90p. What is the cheapest way to buy exactly 10 pens? | Two packs of 5, for £4 | Five packs of 2, for £4.50 | One pack of each kind, £2.90 | Three packs of 5, for £6
exercise: Juice: a 250 ml carton is 40p, a 1 litre bottle is £1.50 and a 2 litre bottle is £3.20. Which is cheapest per litre? | The 1 litre bottle | The 2 litre bottle | The 250 ml cartons | All three cost the same
exercise: Leo buys a comic at £1.35 and a drink at 85p and pays with a £5 note. What change should he get? | £2.80 | £3.80 | £2.20 | £2.70
exercise: What is the smallest number of coins that makes £2.80? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 6
exercise: A toy costs £6.35. What change do you get from £10? | £3.65 | £4.65 | £3.75 | £3.35
exercise: The bill is £4.30. Sam hands over £5.30 instead of £5. Why is that clever? | He gets back a single £1 coin | He gets back 70p in three coins | He pays 30p less for the toy | He gets back £1.30 in two coins
exercise: A £40 coat is half price, and then a further quarter is taken off the new price. What does it cost? | £15 | £10 | £25 | £5
exercise: Books are £6 each on "buy one, get the second half price". What do two books cost? | £9 | £6 | £12 | £3
exercise: Shop A has drinks at £1.50 on "3 for 2"; Shop B sells them at £1.10 each. You need just 2 drinks. Which is cheaper, and by how much? | Shop B, by 80p | Shop A, by 30p | Shop B, by 30p | They cost the same
exercise: Rice: 400 g for £2 or 1 kg for £4.50. Which is cheaper per 100 g? | The 1 kg bag, at 45p | The 400 g bag, at 50p | Both cost 50p per 100 g | The 400 g bag, at 40p
exercise: A £15 game has a fifth off. What does it cost now? | £12 | £10 | £13 | £3
exercise: On a £30 jumper, which saves more: "a tenth off" or "£4 off"? | £4 off, by £1 | A tenth off, by £1 | Both save the same money | A tenth off, by £26
exercise: A TV costs £300 with "£5 off for every £100". How much is saved? | £15 | £5 | £35 | £3
exercise: Dad pays for £13.60 of shopping with a £20 note. What is the fewest notes and coins he can get back? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 6
exercise: Which deal is the odd one out on the price of one bar? | 10 for £4.50 | 6 for £2.40 | 4 for £1.60 | 2 for 80p
exercise: Amy buys a £3.75 book with a £10 note and gets £7.25 back. Is that right? | No, £1 too much: it should be £6.25 | Yes, that is exactly the right change | No, £1 too little: it should be £8.25 | No, 50p too much: it should be £6.75
exercise: What do three items at £1.99 each cost altogether? | £5.97 | £5.99 | £5.67 | £6.03
test: ★★★ Kai from Peckham: "bruv, pens are 5 for £2 or 2 for 90p, I need 7 innit." What is the cheapest way to get at least 7 pens? | One pack of 5 and one of 2, £2.90 | Two packs of 5, for £4 | Four packs of 2, for £3.60 | Three packs of 2, for £2.70
test: ★★★ Jade: "that queue was a mile long, took forever, anyway paid £10 for £7.40 of stuff and she gave me £2.60. Sound?" Is the change right? | Yes, £2.60 is right | No, it should be £3.60 | No, it should be £2.40 | No, it should be £3.40
test: ★★★ Ollie: "Nan gave me £5 - no wait, a tenner - and I got a mag for £3.20." What change did he get? | £6.80 | £1.80 | £7.20 | £6.20
test: ★★★ Zoe: "half off then another quarter off, that's three quarters off, so the £40 jacket's a tenner, no cap." Is she right? | No: half leaves £20, then a quarter off leaves £15 | Yes: three quarters off £40 really is £10 | No: it all comes to £25, a quarter off £40 | No: it comes to £30, only a quarter off
test: ★★★ Dad looks at "2 for £5" on cereal that is £2.30 a box and says, "Hmm, not ideal." What does he really mean? | The deal costs 40p more than two single boxes | The deal is just a bit cheaper, by 40p | The deal costs exactly the same as singles | The deal costs 70p more than singles
test: ★★★ Mia in Perth: "servo's got drinks $3 each or 4 for $10, I need 5, eh." What is the cheapest for exactly 5? | $13 | $15 | $12.50 | $10
test: ★★★ A Geordie gran: "which voucher's the canny one for the £20 toy, pet?" Which saves the most? | A quarter off, saving £5 | A fifth off, saving £5 | £4 off, saving £4 | A tenth off, saving £10
test: ★★★ Finn: "I'm skint, mate, give us 85p back in the fewest coins." How many coins is that? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 2
test: ★★★ Megan from Cardiff: "Shopping was £6.35, I paid with £20 and she gave me £14.65, lush!" What really happened? | She got £1 too much and should give it back | She got exactly the right change | She got £1 too little and should ask for it | She got 35p too much and can keep the rest
test: ★★★ Callum from Glasgow: "Wee bag's 200 g for 90p, big bag's 500 g for £2.40, the big one's aye cheaper." Is he right? | No: the wee bag is 45p per 100 g, the big one 48p | Yes: the big bag is cheaper at 45p per 100 g | They both work out at 48p per 100 g | Yes: big bags are always the cheaper ones
test: ★★★ Ava: "both books are £8, buy one get the second half price, so I pay £8, bussin'." What does she really pay? | £12 | £8 | £4 | £16
exam: It is given that a coat of forty pounds is reduced by one half and thereafter by one quarter of the reduced price. Ascertain the final price. | Fifteen pounds | Ten pounds | Twenty-five pounds | Five pounds
exam: Determine the least number of coins by which change of two pounds eighty pence may be tendered. | Four | Three | Five | Six
exam: Of cartons of 250 millilitres at forty pence, a bottle of one litre at one pound fifty and a bottle of two litres at three pounds twenty, identify the cheapest per litre. | The bottle of one litre | The two-litre bottle | The cartons | All three are equal
exam: It is given that five pounds is deducted for every hundred pounds of the price of a television of three hundred pounds. Ascertain the saving. | Fifteen pounds | Five pounds | Thirty-five pounds | Three pounds
